From 9a3c5e17b2d4f68c0e1a3b5d7f9012345678abcd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MiaAI_Lab <mia.ailab1@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 22:40:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] glm-cuda: TF_GLM_MM_CUBLAS, a prompt's dense linears through
 cuBLASLt

A prompt's dense projections run glm_mm's own prompt kernel, warp-level mma.sync. On a Jetson AGX Thor (sm_110) the SM
is the datacenter Blackwell design and keeps tcgen05/TMEM, the 5th-gen tensor core; cuBLAS dispatches it, the engine's
kernels cannot. Measured on this pair over glm-mm-bench's eight GLM shapes: 1.16 ms against the kernel's 6.41 at 512
rows (5.5x), 0.81 against 1.80 at 128 (2.2x).

TF_GLM_MM_CUBLAS=1 keeps every dense linear's rows as a row-major bf16 [n][k] matrix beside its tiles (one arena, the
bf16 size of the dense weights) and sends a prompt's matmuls (128 rows and up, one K slice) to cuBLASLt through the
binding the vision tower already uses. Off (the default) nothing is copied and the kernels run as they were. Decode
rows stay on the kernels: a cuBLASLt call a linear a token is host overhead the kernels do not pay, and at 8 rows the
1.6x it shows in isolation was 17.8 -> 9.6 tok/s in practice.

cuBLAS accumulates in fp32 in its own order, so the sums are not bit-identical to glm_mm's: the replies differ in the
last bits, which is the trade the switch makes.

